          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.12,
            0.885,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can also write this as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Multiply and check that you get 72 back!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.166,
            0.885,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe how many times each prime factor occurs in the factorisation of 72."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.211,
            0.887,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Compare it with how many times it occurs in the factorisations of 12 and 6 put together."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.269,
            0.196,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.167,
                0.269,
                0.196,
                0.29
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.268,
            0.358,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.297,
            0.884,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Find the prime factorisations of the following numbers: 64, 104, 105, 243, 320, 141, 1728, 729, 1024, 1331, 1000."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.344,
            0.884,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. The prime factorisation of a number has one 2, two 3s, and one 11. What is the number?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.389,
            0.883,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Find three prime numbers, all less than 30, whose product is 1955."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.411,
            0.885,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Find the prime factorisation of these numbers without multiplying first"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.457,
            0.613,
            0.477
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}\\text { a. } 56 \\times 25 \\quad \\text { b. } 108 \\times 75 \\quad \\text { c. } 1000 \\times 81\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.48,
            0.84,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. What is the smallest number whose prime factorisation has:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.503,
            0.594,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a. three different prime numbers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.525,
            0.585,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b. four different prime numbers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.551,
            0.886,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime factorisation is of fundamental importance in the study of numbers. Let us discuss two ways in which it can be useful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.609,
            0.886,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using prime factorisation to check if two numbers are co-prime",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let us again take the numbers 56 and 63. How can we check if they are co-prime? We can use the prime factorisation of both numbers —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.342,
            0.681,
            0.706,
            0.701
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "56 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 7 \\;  and  \\; 63 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.708,
            0.884,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, we see that 7 is a prime factor of 56 as well as 63. Therefore, 56 and 63 are not co-prime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.753,
            0.883,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about 80 and 63? Their prime factorisations are as follows:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.78,
            0.725,
            0.8
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "80 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 5 \\mathrm{~and~} 63 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.807,
            0.885,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are no common prime factors. Can we conclude that they are co-prime? Suppose they have a common factor that is composite. Would the prime factors of this composite common factor appear in the prime factorisation of 80 and 63?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.941,
            0.116,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": []
        },
